WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.237
Public key infrastructure is the trust framework that makes web service authentication scale.

00:00:05.237 --> 00:00:08.399
Server certificates, certificate authorities, intermediate chains, trust stores,

00:00:08.399 --> 00:00:10.374
revocation mechanisms, certificate transparency practices,

00:00:10.374 --> 00:00:15.116
and browser or operating system policies all contribute to the decision a

00:00:15.116 --> 00:00:18.278
client makes when it accepts a TLS connection.

00:00:18.278 --> 00:00:23.415
For a web service, the certificate is not just a file on disk;

00:00:23.415 --> 00:00:26.972
it is part of a larger ecosystem of trust.

00:00:26.972 --> 00:00:29.738
Digital signatures are central to that ecosystem.

00:00:29.738 --> 00:00:32.504
Certificates are signed, certificate chains are verified,

00:00:32.504 --> 00:00:35.665
and clients must understand the signature algorithms involved.

00:00:35.665 --> 00:00:39.222
Post-quantum signature algorithms can introduce larger signatures or certificates,

00:00:39.222 --> 00:00:43.964
and that may affect handshake size, network behavior, memory usage, embedded clients,

00:00:43.964 --> 00:00:45.940
legacy devices, middleboxes, and logging.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:49.891
The concern is not only whether an algorithm is strong;

00:00:49.891 --> 00:00:53.843
it is whether the surrounding ecosystem can use it reliably.

00:00:53.843 --> 00:00:56.214
Certificate migration depends on many parties.

00:00:56.214 --> 00:01:00.166
Browsers, operating systems, certificate authorities, TLS libraries, hardware security modules,

00:01:00.166 --> 00:01:03.722
cloud platforms, device vendors, enterprise trust stores, mobile platforms,

00:01:03.722 --> 00:01:06.489
and security inspection products all influence readiness.

00:01:06.489 --> 00:01:09.650
A service owner may be eager to move,

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:14.392
but if clients cannot validate the chain or a managed platform does

00:01:14.392 --> 00:01:16.368
not support the needed options,

00:01:16.368 --> 00:01:19.134
production migration may be blocked or risky.

00:01:19.134 --> 00:01:22.295
It also helps to separate related use cases.

00:01:22.295 --> 00:01:24.666
Code signing, service identity, device identity,

00:01:24.666 --> 00:01:27.828
and web server certificates all use digital signatures,

00:01:27.828 --> 00:01:31.779
but they have different validation paths, lifetimes, and operational consequences.

00:01:31.779 --> 00:01:35.731
Teams should track ecosystem readiness before changing production certificate strategy.

00:01:35.731 --> 00:01:40.078
The near-term planning move is to inventory certificate use, understand dependencies,

00:01:40.078 --> 00:01:44.820
follow reputable guidance, and avoid one-off certificate choices that clients cannot handle.
